    {
      "paper_id": "doi:10.1086/739286",
      "sequence": 3,
      "source_sequence": 355,
      "article_type": "research-article",
      "title_en": "Externalities of Policy-Induced Scrappage: The Case of Automotive Safety Inspections",
      "title_cn": "政策诱导的车辆报废外部性：以汽车安全检查为例",
      "authors": [
        "Connor R. Forsythe",
        "Akshaya Jha",
        "Jeremy J. Michalek",
        "Kate S. Whitefoot"
      ],
      "abstract_en": "Many transportation policies indirectly affect travel and resulting externalities by inducing changes in vehicle scrappage rates. We leverage the staggered removal of state-level safety inspection programs across the United States to estimate the effects of policy-induced changes in fleet size on travel and fuel use. Using a first-stage difference-in-differences model, we estimate that the removal of safety inspection requirements causes a 5% increase in fleet size on average. Using a second-stage instrumental variables framework robust to weak instruments, we can reject fleet-size elasticities of fleet travel distance above 0.43 and fleet-size elasticities of fleet gasoline consumption above 0.10. Our estimates suggest that assuming fleet-size elasticities of 1 would overestimate the total national externality costs of removing safety inspections by at least 150%, amounting to a cumulative national aggregate error from 1970 to 2017 exceeding $90 billion.",
      "abstract_cn": "许多交通政策通过诱导车辆报废率的变化，间接影响出行及由此产生的外部性。我们利用美国各州逐步取消安全检查项目的自然实验，来估计政策导致的车辆保有量变化对出行和燃料使用的影响。通过第一阶段的双重差分模型，我们估计取消安全检查要求平均导致车辆保有量增加5%。使用对弱工具变量稳健的第二阶段工具变量框架，我们能够拒绝车队行驶距离的保有量弹性高于0.43以及车队汽油消耗的保有量弹性高于0.10的假设。我们的估计表明，假设保有量弹性为1将高估取消安全检查的总国家外部性成本至少150%，相当于从1970年到2017年的累计国家总误差超过$90亿美元。",
      "abstract_snippet_en": "",
